            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"NOVEL\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus understands the Sabbath day primarily as a theological type or symbol pointing to spiritual rest in Christ, eschatological peace, a covenant sign of loyalty, or a memorial of redemption.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines the Sabbath linguistically and structurally as the 'marked member' of a sabbath-unit (paralleling the new moon day of a month), noting that when doubled ('sabbath of rest') it refers to the resting member itself, whether a weekly seventh day, the Day of Atonement, or a seventh year.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A designated time of sacred rest symbolizing spiritual, creational, or eschatological peace and redemption.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The structurally marked resting member of a calendrical unit.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"NO_CONSENSUS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"NO_BASELINE\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus focuses on the theological and typological meaning of the Sabbath, whereas the book provides a structural and lexical definition of the Hebrew terminology.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None explicitly, but the book's structural categorization of time-units and their 'marked members' (extending the terminology to the Day of Atonement and sabbatical years through lexical doubling) is entirely absent from the consensus's symbolic catalog.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's focus on the lexical distinction between a time-unit and its marked resting member introduces a structural framework that does not appear in the blind consensus, making it a novel identification.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's definition is purely lexical and calendrical; it operates on a different conceptual level than the consensus and might not preclude the theological symbolism the consensus emphasizes.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
